   * Thread pool used by parallel streams [02:38]
Yes. Parallel Streams use the ForkJoinPool.commonPool() by default.

Example
List<Integer> numbers = List.of(1, 2, 3, 4, 5);

numbers.parallelStream()
       .forEach(n -> System.out.println(
           Thread.currentThread().getName() + " : " + n
       ));

Possible output:

ForkJoinPool.commonPool-worker-1 : 2
ForkJoinPool.commonPool-worker-3 : 5
ForkJoinPool.commonPool-worker-2 : 1
main : 4
ForkJoinPool.commonPool-worker-1 : 3

Notice the thread names like:

ForkJoinPool.commonPool-worker-1
ForkJoinPool.commonPool-worker-2

This shows that parallelStream() is using the common ForkJoinPool.

Why does it use ForkJoinPool?

A parallel stream:

Splits the collection into smaller chunks (fork).
Processes those chunks in parallel using worker threads.
Combines the results (join).

This divide-and-conquer approach is exactly what ForkJoinPool is designed for.

Interview Question

Q: Which thread pool is used by parallelStream()?
By default, parallelStream() uses the ForkJoinPool.commonPool(), a shared thread pool that uses a work-stealing algorithm to efficiently execute tasks in parallel.

Can we use our own ForkJoinPool?
Yes. If you don't want to use the common pool, you can submit the parallel stream to a custom ForkJoinPool:

ForkJoinPool pool = new ForkJoinPool(4);

pool.submit(() ->
    list.parallelStream()
        .forEach(System.out::println)
).join();

This limits the parallelism to 4 worker threads instead of using the common pool.

