Singleton ensures that only one instance of a class is created throughout the application and provides a global access point to that instance.
To save memory.
When only one shared object is needed.
example
Logger
Configuration Manager
By default, Spring Beans are Singleton scoped. When we annotate a class with @Service, @Component, or @Repository, Spring creates only one instance 
of that bean and reuses it across the application.

Interview Answer

Singleton pattern ensures only one object of a class is created during the application's lifetime. It is useful when a single shared instance is sufficient,
such as configuration classes or service beans. In Spring Boot, beans are singleton by default.

2. Factory Pattern
Factory Pattern creates objects without exposing the object creation logic to the client. The client only asks for an object, and the factory decides which implementation to create.

Suppose you have

Payment

CardPayment

UPIPayment

NetBankingPayment

Instead of writing

new CardPayment();

you do

Payment payment = PaymentFactory.getPayment("CARD");

The factory decides which object to create.

Spring IOC Container itself acts like a Factory because it creates and manages beans.
Factory Pattern encapsulates object creation logic. Instead of creating objects directly using the new keyword,
the client requests the factory to create the required object. This improves flexibility and reduces tight coupling.

3. Abstract Factory Pattern 
Factory creates one type of object.
Abstract Factory creates families of related objects.

Abstract Factory provides an interface for creating families of related objects without specifying their concrete classes.
It is useful when multiple related objects need to be created together.

4. Prototype Pattern 
Prototype Pattern creates new objects by cloning an existing object instead of creating a new one from scratch.
Prototype Pattern creates new objects by cloning existing objects. It is useful when object creation is expensive or when many similar objects need to be created.

5. Builder Pattern 
Builder Pattern is used to create complex objects step by step.

Example

Instead of

Employee e = new Employee(
"Akhila",
27,
"Hyderabad",
90000,
true,
...
);

Use

Employee employee =
Employee.builder()
       .name("Akhila")
       .age(27)
       .city("Hyderabad")
       .salary(90000)
       .build();

Much more readable.

Lombok
@Builder

uses the Builder Pattern.

Builder Pattern is used to construct complex objects step by step. It improves code readability and avoids constructors with many parameters.
In Spring Boot projects, we commonly use Lombok's @Builder annotation to implement this pattern.
